;;; An hrule's `:assert` splits a name that starts with `or` —
;;; regression `kw-or-in-hrule` (`corpus/regressions.toml`): a reserved word is a
;;; keyword only as a whole token (Q-M1e2.17).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(p a)`, a relation `orz`, and an hrule, `h`, proposing `(orz
;;; ?x)` for each `(p ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `01-grammar.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `orz` is a name like any other. H is `(orz
;;; a)` and R is ∅ and {(orz a)}: the answer of `kw-or-in-hrule-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; At `35186bc0` the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read the template `(orz ?x)` as `(or
;;; z ?x)`, so H was `(or z a)`, a fact of the reserved `or`, and R was ∅ and
;;; {(or z a)}. (`ein solve FILE` at `35186bc0`, release and debug; every mode
;;; and `ein oracle` the same.) A keyword is a whole word now, at the boundary
;;; `01-grammar.md` § 1 states (Q-M1e2.17), and the program answers its twin's R.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?3}") (relation orz Node :why "orz holds of {?3}")
(p a)
(hrule h () :match (p ?x) :assert (orz ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}: guess that orz holds of {?x} too")
